1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Open a synthetic recording in Audacity through its native file dialog.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The 12-second mono source contains a DC offset and low-frequency rumble.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Select the complete 12-second recording.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Open High-Pass Filter from Audacity's built-in effects.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Apply a 100 Hz high-pass filter with a 24 dB-per-octave roll-off.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Remove DC offset and normalize the peak amplitude to -1.0 dB.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Apply normalization to the entire selected track.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The processed waveform is centered and fills a larger peak range.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Prepare a local WAV export of the cleaned audio.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Export all 12 seconds as mono, 48 kHz, signed 16-bit PCM.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Name the native Audacity project audio-qc.aup3.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Save the processed audio as a local AUP3 project.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Close the saved project, then reopen its local AUP3 file.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The reopened project retains the centered, normalized 12-second waveform.
